I am using ryzenadj for adjusting CPU Wattage. There is no undervolting/curvesetting, just limiting the max usable wattage. 
The two scripts Adjust_CPU_Wattage.sh (keybind to Super+P)
and Apply_last_CPU_Wattage.sh (runs as login)

both scripts need root permission to run. But in practice, prompting the user for password each time is very retarded. That is why, a polkit rule has been added for these two scripts ONLY.
The polkit rule can be located in the file: /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/90-ryzen-power.rules 
This file has rules added so that user "aashu" can execute both commands without asking for password.
BUT, to execute these, both scripts need to be run with "pkexec" appended at the start of the scripts.

The keybind for changing the CPU Wattage is:
Super+P = "pkexec /mnt/ENJOY_VOLUME/Customization/Scripts/Adjust_CPU_Wattage.sh"
and the entry for the command to run ar boot is also : "pkexec /mnt/ENJOY_VOLUME/Customization/Scripts/Apply_last_CPU_Wattage.sh"

To move these both scripts you will have to edit all of these. 

NOTES:
sudoers file has not been edited for this.
the NOPASSWD: /path/to/script was tried and failed to do a clean job, aka, very hacky and messy solution that ends up in breaking sudo for EVERY other command.
The ryzenadj binary was obtained from UTUUNIX or something like that, like x86UniversalTuningUtilityForUnix's tarball. I extracted it and found the the binary for ryzenadj in the dependecies build folder. So it is what it is.

A copy of the ryzenadj binary is also stored at /mnt/Customization/Apps_Installed/executables/ryzenadj

